# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_config_precision.py" "CONFIG PRECISION GATE: PASS" "config-coupling precision (ubiquitous-key false-coupling, recall-safe)" "config-coupling precision: the CONFIG graph couples files that read the SAME config key (a real coupling -- two files both reading STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET ARE entangled), but UBIQUITOUS keys (timeout / log.level / max_size / config.default / status) appear in nearly every config+file, so two UNRELATED files both reading 'timeout' are NOT coupled -- the config analogue of the proven ubiquitous-CALL-name fix. MEASURED on real repos (airflow/netbox/redash): ubiquitous-key-ONLY couplings co-change ~3x LESS than specific-key couplings (mean lift 2.36 vs 7.79 on airflow) and demoting ~99k such pairs cost 0 real recall. RECALL IS SACRED: _cg_config._specific_key suppresses a config_key ONLY when EVERY word-token is generic (no distinguishing token anywhere); a key with even one specific token (webhook in webhook_secret, pool in db.pool_size, stripe in stripe.api_key) is KEPT -- when in doubt, KEEP. content-free (key NAMES only, never values). This gate proves on synthetic configs+code: (A) ubiquitous-only readers are NOT config-coupled; (B/C) specific keys -- incl. compounds with a generic LEAF like WEBHOOK_SECRET/REDIS_URL -- ARE kept and couple their readers; (D) a file reading both still couples via the SPECIFIC key only; (E) DATA-DUMP / FIXTURE / SEED / LOCALE files (Django fixtures, populatedb_data.json seed dumps, fixtures/ + locale/ trees, .po catalogs) mint NO config_key, so two unrelated files mentioning the same model FIELD NAME do NOT false-couple -- audit on real Django/Rails repos found 65-91 percent of config couples were these fixture-field-name false couples, and config shares the same contested-to-warn-to-pause path as schema so a false config couple causes a false PAUSE -- while a REAL settings.yaml / app.json sitting next to a fixture keeps every genuine key (recall preserved)" "config precision: ubiquitous-key-only config couplings (timeout/log.level/max_size) are demoted at extraction as spurious (measured 3x lower co-change, 0 recall cost on real repos), AND data-dump / fixture / seed / locale files are skipped at mint (audit: 65-91 percent of config couples on real Django/Rails repos were fixture-field-name false couples that share schema's pause path), while every specific app-config key -- including generic-leaf compounds like webhook_secret -- is kept; recall-safe, content-free" 18
